      "title": "Image-space GPU aggregation for real-time visual scale",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Shared Pattern",
          "body": "Several sources describe real-time graphics pipelines that trade exact per-element rendering for perceptually or analytically useful aggregation. In large graph rendering, density-based node aggregation, edge cumulation, pixel-bin seed points, and a two-pass accumulation field reduce work while preserving the density field for a viewport. Image-based crowd rendering similarly uses impostors, compressed textures, compact frame storage, and directional or positional discretization to keep dense animated crowds interactive."
        },
        {
          "title": "GPU And Image-Space Execution",
          "body": "The shared concept is not a single algorithm but a design pattern: move expensive visual structure into screen-space, image-space, or precomputed image representations that fit GPU rendering workflows. This pattern appears in OpenGL graph level-of-detail rendering, image-based crowd impostors, CUDA-assisted edge bundling, and foveated rendering's regional quality allocation."
        },
        {
          "title": "Scope And Caveats",
          "body": "The evidence supports a topic focused on scale management, with caveats that each source optimizes a different visual object: graph nodes and edges, animated crowds, bundled connectivity, or perceptual display regions. The common synthesis is therefore about practical real-time rendering strategies rather than a single interchangeable technique."
        }
