      "title": "Fields and local structure as guides for visual aggregation",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Field-Guided Aggregation",
          "body": "The sources repeatedly use fields, sampled spaces, or local geometric summaries to decide how visual elements should move, merge, or be rendered. Large-graph level-of-detail rendering treats node density as a height field and hill-climbs edge endpoints toward cluster maxima, while skeleton-based edge bundling constructs distance-field shapes and attracts edges toward skeleton paths."
        },
        {
          "title": "Shader And Distance-Field Parallels",
          "body": "Shadercoding and raymarching provide a related but broader rendering concept: spatial or distance-field descriptions can generate image structure inside fragment shaders. The connection is conceptual rather than identical, because the graph papers use fields for aggregation and layout readability, while shadercoding uses mathematical fields for image synthesis and live visual construction."
        },
        {
          "title": "Synthesis Boundary",
          "body": "This topic can synthesize how spatial abstractions turn raw geometry or graph data into controllable visual structure. The evidence supports comparing density fields, feature transforms, skeleton paths, and signed distance fields as field-like mechanisms, while distinguishing their different goals in graph readability and image generation."
        }
