        {
          "title": "Overview Before Detail",
          "body": "Large visual datasets are made useful by combining overview summaries with interaction mechanisms that let users recover selected detail. LaGO supports zooming, panning, normalization controls, color mapping, and detail-on-demand labels, and its examples show recognizable patterns in flights, migration, Net50, and OpenStreetMap data."
        },
        {
          "title": "Selective Fidelity",
          "body": "This connects to skeleton-based edge bundling's coarse-scale connectivity focus and to foveated rendering's principle of allocating quality unevenly according to perceptual need. In each case, the visualization is intentionally not uniform: it emphasizes structures likely to matter while accepting some loss, reduction, or quality variation elsewhere."
        },
        {
          "title": "Interpretation Limits",
          "body": "The topic should frame these systems as interactive summary interfaces, not as lossless data displays. The evidence explicitly warns that bundling can obscure individual edges and distort directions, and foveation similarly depends on perceptual assumptions that guide where quality can safely be reduced."
        }
